      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To convert a point from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we need to use the following formulas:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta = \\arctan(\\frac{y}{x})\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\arccos(\\frac{z}{\\rho})\n\\]\n\nGiven the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\),\n\\[\nx = 0, \\quad y = -3\\sqrt{3}, \\quad z = 3\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(\\rho\\):\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\nNext, calculate \\(\\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\nSince \\(\\tan(\\theta)\\) is undefined when \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y < 0\\), we use the fact that the point lies in the negative \\(y\\)-axis:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\n\\]\nFinally, calculate \\(\\phi\\):\n\\[\n\\phi = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\arccos\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\nSince \\(\\arccos\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince we know \\(\\rho\\) and \\(\\phi\\) and \\(\\theta\\) is a multiple of \\(\\pi\\), the valid spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}\n\\]",
